---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import AccGroup from '../../../components/AccGroup.astro';
import RelatedEquipment from '../../../components/RelatedEquipment.astro';
import ModelStatus from '../../../components/ModelStatus.astro';
import { DJM_900NXS } from '../../../data/facts.js';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';

/**
 * DJM-900NXS, the FIRST nexus generation. Not the DJM-900NXS2 and not the
 * DJM-900SRT. Structure follows djm-900nxs2.astro, hedging follows
 * cdj-900nxs.astro, because this page has the same editorial problem: a
 * product whose published firmware history stopped years ago.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE STRONGEST FACT ON ANY MIXER PAGE HERE. Below
 * firmware 1.28 the mixer is documented to shut itself down, in
 * AlphaTheta's own words, "an infrequently-encountered issue where
 * DJM-900NXS unexpectedly shuts down". On a mixer that was the club
 * standard for years, and is still bolted into rooms nobody has updated,
 * that is the entire master output going away mid-set. Second: below 1.32
 * it can refuse to LEAVE standby when the wake button is pressed, so a
 * desk that slept between sets can look dead on the stand.
 *
 * DO NOT SAY THE FIRMWARE LINE IS CLOSED FOREVER. 1.32 is from November
 * 2016 and is still the offered download, which is nearly ten years, but
 * AlphaTheta publishes no end-of-support statement, so "ended" is an
 * INFERENCE FROM SILENCE and this page writes it as an observation. The
 * CDJ-900NXS page said "permanently" until 2026-08-07 and was corrected,
 * because this site's own data undercuts the prediction: the CDJ-2000NXS
 * shipped a version in 2024 after six and a half years of silence.
 *
 * THREE BEHAVIOURS ON THIS DESK LOOK LIKE FAULTS AND ARE NOT, and they are
 * why the page exists: Beat FX cue outputs DRY sound on four of the
 * effects by design, Peak Limiter is ON by default after an update, and
 * QUANTIZE silently does not apply to four of the Beat FX.
 *
 * NO USB STORAGE ROLE AT ALL. Firmware goes in from a computer over a LAN
 * cable into the LINK port, not from a USB stick like every other Pioneer
 * unit on this site, so exFAT and NTFS are NOT APPLICABLE here rather than
 * unsupported. It is the one unit in this reference where a DJ never has
 * to think about drive format.
 *
 * WHERE THE OFFICIAL RECORD CONTRADICTS ITSELF, BOTH SIDES ARE PUBLISHED.
 * A/D bit depth is 24-bit in the manual and 32-bit on the product page.
 * The page attributes both and says the documents disagree rather than
 * picking a winner. Same treatment for the operating system support list.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT DOCUMENTED IS SAID TO BE NOT DOCUMENTED: the auto standby
 * timeout, its shipped default and how to disable it, plus dimensions,
 * weight and the electrical specifications, none of which AlphaTheta
 * publishes on the web for this model. No figure is borrowed from another
 * mixer to fill those holes.
 *
 * Every version number, effect list and source URL comes from DJM_900NXS
 * in facts.js and is rendered from it. The two short lists the constant
 * does not hold are declared once below, for the same reason.
 */
const UPDATED = DJM_900NXS.checked;
// Both the floor and the ceiling, the way FLOOR works on the CDJ-900NXS page:
// there is no higher version to reach for.
const FLOOR = DJM_900NXS.newestKnown;
const FX = DJM_900NXS.soundColorFx.join(', ');
const NO_QUANTIZE = DJM_900NXS.quantizeExcludedFx.join(', ');
const NO_QUANTIZE_N = DJM_900NXS.quantizeExcludedFx.length;
// The four Beat FX that cue DRY. DJM_900NXS holds no key for this list, so it
// is declared once here and rendered from, never retyped into prose. The
// change shipped inside the combined 1.21 to 1.26 update block, which is why
// the version rendered beside it is rollVolumeFix.
const CUE_DRY = ['MELODIC', 'ROLL', 'REV ROLL', 'SLIP ROLL'];
const CUE_DRY_LIST = CUE_DRY.join(', ');
const CUE_DRY_N = CUE_DRY.length;
const CUE_DRY_FROM = DJM_900NXS.rollVolumeFix;
// Where the published history begins. Nothing below this version is listed
// anywhere, so the page names the edge of the record instead of calling the
// record complete. Also not a key in DJM_900NXS.
const HISTORY_STARTS_AT = '1.20';
// The file systems that are NOT APPLICABLE rather than unsupported, because
// no drive is ever inserted into this mixer.
const NOT_APPLICABLE_FS = ['exFAT', 'NTFS'].join(' and ');
// The three PRO DJ LINK functions AlphaTheta names for this mixer. Also not a
// key in DJM_900NXS, so it is declared once and rendered from twice.
const LINK_FUNCTIONS = ['LINK MONITOR', 'STATUS INFORMATION', 'QUANTIZE'];
const LINK_FUNCTIONS_LIST = `${LINK_FUNCTIONS.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${LINK_FUNCTIONS.at(-1)}`;
// Driver versions and dates from the official download pages. Kept together so
// the two numbers and the two dates cannot drift apart in prose.
const MAC_DRIVER = { version: '4.0.2', date: '18 May 2023', floor: '4.0.0' };
const WIN_DRIVER = { version: '1.810', date: '9 December 2021', floor: '1.710' };
// The A/D figure AlphaTheta publishes in two places, differently.
const AD_MANUAL = '24-bit';
const AD_PRODUCT = '32-bit';
const DA_MANUAL = '32-bit';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/knowledge/pioneer-dj/djm-900nxs#article',
  headline: 'DJM-900NXS: the operational reference',
  description: `What every DJ should know before working a DJM-900NXS: it is documented to shut itself down below firmware ${DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix}, standby sometimes could not be cancelled below ${FLOOR}, Beat FX cue outputs dry sound on ${CUE_DRY_N} effects by design, Peak Limiter is on by default after an update, and there is no USB storage role at all.`,
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-08-07',
  dateModified: '2026-08-07',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/knowledge/pioneer-dj/djm-900nxs#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/images/djm-900nxs-panel-full.webp',
};
---
<Base
  title={`DJM-900NXS: Shutdown Below Firmware ${DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix} | Save My Gig`}
  description={`The DJM-900NXS field manual: it shut itself down below firmware ${DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix}, standby could stick below ${FLOOR}, and Beat FX cue is dry by design.`}
  path="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/djm-900nxs"
  crumbName="DJM-900NXS"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/knowledge">Knowledge base</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj">Pioneer DJ / AlphaTheta</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span>DJM-900NXS</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="hw-head">
      <div class="hw-head-text">
        <ModelStatus slug="djm-900nxs" checked={UPDATED} />
        <h1 class="rb-h1">DJM-900NXS, <span class="accent">the field manual</span></h1>
      </div>
      <a
        class="hw-thumb"
        href="#djm-900nxs-lightbox"
        aria-label="Open a larger photo of the DJM-900NXS control panel"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/djm-900nxs-panel-thumb.webp"
          alt="Pioneer DJ DJM-900NXS four-channel DJ mixer control panel, top view"
          width="426"
          height="500"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="lightbox" id="djm-900nxs-lightbox">
      <a href="#" class="lightbox-backdrop" aria-label="Close"></a>
      <a href="#" class="lightbox-close" aria-label="Close">&times;</a>
      <img
        src="/images/djm-900nxs-panel-full.webp"
        srcset="/images/djm-900nxs-panel-800.webp 800w, /images/djm-900nxs-panel-full.webp 1192w"
        sizes="92vw"
        alt="Pioneer DJ DJM-900NXS four-channel DJ mixer control panel, top view"
        width="1192"
        height="1400"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <input type="checkbox" id="sv-toggle" class="sv-toggle sr-only" />
      <p class="sv-text" style="margin:0">
        <strong>Booth briefing. Below firmware {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix} this mixer
        is documented to shut itself down.</strong> AlphaTheta's own wording is
        "Fixed an infrequently-encountered issue where DJM-900NXS unexpectedly
        shuts down", and on a desk still bolted into rooms nobody has updated that
        is the whole master output going away mid-set. Below {FLOOR} standby can
        also refuse to cancel, so a mixer that slept between sets looks dead on the
        stand. Read the version off the unit before you play. Three more behaviours
        on this desk look like faults and are not, and they are the sections
        directly below.
      </p>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more">Read more…</label>
      <label for="sv-toggle" class="sv-more sv-more-open">Read less</label>
    </div>

    <AccGroup icon="zap" title="Emergency fixes" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="unexpected-shutdown">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The mixer switched itself off mid-set</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Documented, and fixed in
        {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix}, published 26 November 2012. AlphaTheta's wording
        is "Fixed an infrequently-encountered issue where DJM-900NXS unexpectedly
        shuts down". Below that version, the desk switching itself off is a known
        firmware fault rather than a mystery.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> It is the worst place a fault can
        sit. Everything in the booth runs through the mixer, so when the mixer goes
        the room goes with it, and "infrequently-encountered" is exactly why nobody
        finds it at soundcheck. It is also not auto standby: standby is a state you
        wake from with a button, this is the unit powering down without warning.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Power it back on and let it boot. While it
        boots, the players keep playing into a dead desk, so decide where you are
        coming back in rather than reaching for faders blind. Then read the version
        as soon as you have a quiet moment, because it changes what you tell the
        venue afterwards.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> On a desk you control, get it to
        {FLOOR} between bookings. On a desk you do not control, read the version at
        soundcheck, and if it is below {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix} say so to whoever
        runs the room. This is the one item on this page a venue genuinely wants to
        hear about, because the fix is free and the failure takes the whole night
        down.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a> <a href="#read-the-version">Read the version in ten seconds</a> <a href="/emergency">Start Emergency Mode</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="will-not-wake">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">It looks dead on the stand and will not wake up</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Suspect standby before suspecting the
        power supply. There is an auto standby function, the control that cancels
        it is the LFO FORM (WAKE UP) button, and below {DJM_900NXS.wakeUpFix}
        standby "sometimes could not be canceled when the [LFO FORM (WAKE UP)]
        button was pressed". That is AlphaTheta's own description of the fault it
        fixed.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> A desk that sat through a long
        changeover, an overrunning support set or an abandoned soundcheck can put
        itself into standby, and on an older version the documented way back out is
        the thing that intermittently did not work. From the front it reads as a
        mixer that has died.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Press LFO FORM (WAKE UP). If nothing
        happens, the power switch is the fallback, and a restart costs you the
        master for as long as the boot takes, so do it before you need the desk
        rather than in the middle of a transition.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Get the desk to
        {DJM_900NXS.wakeUpFix}, which is the version that fixed this, and touch a
        control during long gaps. What this page will not tell you is how long the
        timeout is or how to switch standby off: AlphaTheta does not publish the
        timeout, the shipped default or the disable procedure for this model
        anywhere we could read, and we are not borrowing a figure from another
        mixer to fill the gap.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#auto-standby">Auto standby, and what is not documented</a> <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="beat-fx-cue-dry">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">I cued a Beat FX and heard no effect</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> That is the documented, intended
        behaviour, not a fault. From {CUE_DRY_FROM} onward, pressing the Beat FX
        CUE button with {CUE_DRY_LIST} selected outputs the original sound with no
        effect on it, whether the effect is switched on or off.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> AlphaTheta changed it deliberately
        in the update block that ends at {CUE_DRY_FROM} and wrote the change into
        the history, so a DJ auditioning one of those {CUE_DRY_N} in headphones and
        hearing a clean signal is hearing the mixer work as specified. It is a
        genuinely confusing design decision, and it costs people a soundcheck every
        time they meet it for the first time.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> There is nothing to fix. Judge those
        {CUE_DRY_N} in the mix rather than in the cue, or bring the effect in with
        the depth control low and open it up once it is live.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Learn which {CUE_DRY_N} they are,
        {CUE_DRY_LIST}, and treat the rest of the Beat FX as normally
        pre-listenable. On a mixer below {CUE_DRY_FROM} the cue behaved
        differently, so a desk you know from one venue can behave differently in
        another.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx">Beat FX, and why the count is 13 or 14</a> <a href="#quantize">QUANTIZE, and where it silently does nothing</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="master-squashed">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The master sounds squashed on this mixer</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Look for PKLIMIT in CLUB SETUP. Peak
        Limiter arrived in firmware {DJM_900NXS.peakLimiterIntro} and AlphaTheta is
        explicit about the consequence: "By updating the firmware, Peak Limiter is
        enabled as default." Factory default is on, so an updated desk is limiting
        the master whether anyone chose that or not.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> The limiter exists to alleviate
        "sudden, unpleasant digital clipping of the master output", so it is doing
        its job. Driven hot, though, it is audible as compression you did not dial
        in, and because it appeared in a firmware update rather than on the front
        panel, almost nobody knows it is there.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Fix the gain first. If the meters are
        living at the top, the limiter is the symptom and your trim is the cause.
        If the levels are sensible and the master still sounds pressed, PKLIMIT in
        CLUB SETUP is the setting, and it is a decision to make deliberately rather
        than a fault to clear.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> In a venue you play often, find out
        what PKLIMIT is set to once and note it, because it survives between your
        visits and it changes how the desk responds to a hot channel.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#peak-limiter">Peak Limiter, explained</a> <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="fx-noise-and-silence">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">An effect is noisy, silent or uneven in level</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Four documented effect faults, every one
        of them fixed in firmware. Some effects "do not work as expected when such
        effects are switched on" below {DJM_900NXS.effectsNotEngagingFix}. The
        volume of ROLL, REV ROLL and SLIP ROLL "is not always stable" below
        {DJM_900NXS.rollVolumeFix}. Effect sound "is not always output when REV
        ROLL is enabled immediately after ROLL is enabled" below
        {DJM_900NXS.revRollSilentFix}. And a slight noise while operating the X-PAD
        below {DJM_900NXS.xpadNoiseFix}.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> These are firmware faults in the
        performance section, which is the part of the desk nobody tests before
        doors. The REV ROLL one in particular only appears when you chain it
        straight out of ROLL, which is exactly how a DJ would use it and not at all
        how anyone checks a mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Change effect. If a roll is dropping level
        or arriving silent, another Beat FX will do the transition, and none of
        these faults touches the channel signal path.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Read the version, then rule those
        effects out for the night rather than discovering which ones behave. On a
        desk you control, {FLOOR} clears all four at once. There is also a separate
        documented noise on the computer side: below {DJM_900NXS.usbNoiseFix}, "a
        noise might be heard in the audio input and output from the USB port on
        some computers", which is a laptop-specific fault rather than an effects
        one.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx">Beat FX</a> <a href="#sound-card">The built-in sound card</a> <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="bpm-display-jumping">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The BPM on the mixer display is jumping around</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Documented, and the cause is on the other
        machine. Below {DJM_900NXS.slipBpmDisplayFix}, "the BPM values on the
        display may randomly fluctuate if SLIP function is used on CDJ-2000NXS or
        CDJ-900 which is linked to DJM-900NXS via PRO DJ LINK". The symptom is on
        the mixer, the trigger is a player.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> It is the classic mixed-booth trap in
        reverse: the fault appears on the device you are looking at, caused by a
        feature being used on a device you are not. Nobody connects a jumping BPM
        readout on the desk with someone using SLIP on a deck.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Nothing in your audio is wrong. Tap the BPM
        in manually or set Beat FX by ear for the rest of that track, and do not
        trust the auto reading while SLIP is in use on a linked player.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> {DJM_900NXS.slipBpmDisplayFix} on
        the mixer clears it. In a booth that mixes generations, this is worth
        knowing before you rely on beat-synced effects sitting on top of another
        DJ's SLIP work.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#compatibility">Players and PRO DJ LINK</a> <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/cdj-2000nxs">CDJ-2000NXS</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="link-not-working">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">PRO DJ LINK is not coming up</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Suspect the hub before the mixer.
        AlphaTheta's own instruction is "Use a 100Base-TX-compatible switching hub.
        Some switching hubs may not operate properly", which is a manufacturer
        saying out loud that the wrong hub is a supported way for this to fail.
        With a hub, up to {DJM_900NXS.maxPlayers} DJ players and
        {DJM_900NXS.maxComputers} computers can be connected.</p>
        <p><strong>Why this happens.</strong> Link on this mixer is a LAN cable
        into the LINK terminal, and anything beyond a single connection goes
        through a hub that is usually whatever the venue had in a drawer. A hub
        that works perfectly for a printer is not necessarily one that carries
        this.</p>
        <p><strong>Fix it now.</strong> Audio does not need the network. Every
        player keeps playing through its own output with the Link cable out, so you
        lose {LINK_FUNCTIONS_LIST} and you do not lose the set. If the room has a
        second hub, swapping it is a
        faster test than reseating cables one by one.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> If you tour with your own booth
        network, carry the hub you know works. In a venue you do not control, plan
        the set so that quantised effects and Link browsing are a bonus rather than
        a dependency.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#compatibility">Players and PRO DJ LINK</a> <a href="#quantize">QUANTIZE</a> <a href="/fix/cdj-error-e-8302">Error E-8302</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="warn" title="Known issues" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="known-issues">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Documented fixes, by firmware version</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Eight published entries, running from
        {HISTORY_STARTS_AT} in June 2011 to {FLOOR} in November 2016.
        <strong>{FLOOR} is both the floor and the ceiling</strong>, because nothing
        has followed it. Every entry below is AlphaTheta's own wording,
        condensed.</p>
        <p><strong>What this list is not.</strong> It is not the complete history
        of the product. Nothing below {HISTORY_STARTS_AT} is published anywhere we
        could read, and versions 1.22 to 1.25, 1.27 and 1.29 are not listed at all,
        so the record has holes in it and we are naming them rather than presenting
        a partial list as a full one.</p>
        <dl class="rb-errors">
          <dt>{FLOOR}</dt><dd>15 November 2016. Standby mode sometimes could not be cancelled when the LFO FORM (WAKE UP) button was pressed.</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.usbNoiseFix}</dt><dd>27 February 2014. "A noise might be heard in the audio input and output from the USB port on some computers."</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.peakLimiterIntro}</dt><dd>16 October 2013. Peak Limiter added, and enabled as default once the firmware is updated. Support for the KUVO service. Some effects did not work as expected when those effects were switched on.</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix}</dt><dd>26 November 2012. "An infrequently-encountered issue where DJM-900NXS unexpectedly shuts down." Also: "the BPM values on the display may randomly fluctuate if SLIP function is used on CDJ-2000NXS or CDJ-900 which is linked to DJM-900NXS via PRO DJ LINK."</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.mountainLionSupport}</dt><dd>19 November 2012. Support for OS X Mountain Lion 10.8.</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.midiClockFix} to {DJM_900NXS.rollVolumeFix}</dt><dd>26 June 2012, published as one combined block covering everything between those two versions. AUTO BPM accuracy improved. From this update, pressing Beat FX CUE with {CUE_DRY_LIST} selected outputs the original sound without the effect, on or off. A slight noise while operating the X-PAD. "The volume level of ROLL, REV ROLL and SLIP ROLL is not always stable." "Effect sound is not always output when REV ROLL is enabled immediately after ROLL is enabled." SOUND COLOR FX button response time improved.</dd>
          <dt>{DJM_900NXS.midiClockFix}</dt><dd>5 October 2011. MIDI timing clock accuracy.</dd>
          <dt>{HISTORY_STARTS_AT}</dt><dd>28 June 2011. Changes to the sound of REVERB, TRANS, MELODIC, SLIP ROLL, DUB ECHO, GATE/COMP and FILTER. AlphaTheta's own text for this entry carries spelling errors, which is worth knowing if you go looking for these words in the PDF.</dd>
        </dl>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Has the firmware line ended.</strong>
        Read this as an observation rather than a promise. {FLOOR} is dated
        15 November 2016 and it is still the download AlphaTheta offers, which is
        nearly ten years, and no end-of-support statement exists for this mixer. So
        "ended" is our inference from silence, not a documented fact, and silence
        has been wrong before: the CDJ-2000NXS received a firmware version in 2024,
        more than six years after the one before it. Plan on nothing more arriving,
        without treating that as settled.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>One detail that makes the timeline more
        interesting than it looks.</strong> {FLOOR} shipped roughly ten months
        after the DJM-900NXS2 was announced, so the two mixers were supported side
        by side rather than one handing over to the other. AlphaTheta names no
        predecessor and no successor for this model.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">
          <strong>Firmware downloads.</strong> <a href={DJM_900NXS.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official AlphaTheta change history, PDF →</a><br />
          <a href={DJM_900NXS.sourceProduct} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official product page →</a><br />
          <span class="dim" style="font-size:0.85rem">Broken link? <a href="/feedback">Let us know</a> and we will fix it.</span>
        </p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/firmware">Every model, by firmware version</a> <a href="#firmware-update">How the update goes in</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sliders" title="Features explained" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="beat-fx">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Beat FX, and why the count is 13 or 14</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Both numbers are right, and they
        reconcile: there are {DJM_900NXS.beatFxCount} effects inside the mixer,
        while the selector has {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions} positions,
        because the extra one is SND/RTN (MIDI LFO), which routes to the SEND and
        RETURN terminals instead of to an internal effect. AlphaTheta's marketing
        counts the effects and the manual's table counts the selector.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> If you have read
        {DJM_900NXS.beatFxCount} in one place and counted
        {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions} on the desk, nothing is wrong with
        either the mixer or the documentation. It also tells you something useful
        about the design: the outboard loop is treated as one of the Beat FX
        rather than as a separate patch bay, so it lands on the same control
        surface as everything else.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Beat FX locks timing to the
        BPM, so an echo or a roll lands musically. The X-PAD is the fast way in,
        and the SOUND COLOR FX button response was improved in the
        {DJM_900NXS.midiClockFix} to {DJM_900NXS.rollVolumeFix} block if you are
        comparing an old desk with a newer one.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Trusting an auto BPM reading without
        checking it. Expecting to pre-listen every effect: {CUE_DRY_N} of them cue
        dry by design. Assuming QUANTIZE applies to all of them, which it does
        not.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx-cue-dry">Cueing an effect and hearing nothing</a> <a href="#quantize">QUANTIZE</a> <a href="#send-return">The send and return loop</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="sound-color-fx">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Sound Color FX</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {DJM_900NXS.soundColorFx.length} effects
        on every channel, one knob each, no menu: {FX}.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> They sit on the channel strip beside a
        {DJM_900NXS.eqBands}-band EQ isolator, so they are fast enough to use
        mid-mix without looking away from the room or the next track.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Filter into a breakdown,
        SPACE to open a track up before a drop, DUB ECHO to hand off the last bar
        of an outgoing record.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Overusing CRUSH or NOISE on a big
        system, they read as dramatic in headphones and can be harsh through a full
        PA. Note also that the sound of several effects, including DUB ECHO and
        GATE/COMP, was changed in {HISTORY_STARTS_AT}, so a very old desk does not
        sound identical to an updated one.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx">Beat FX</a> <a href="#master-squashed">A squashed master</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="quantize">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">QUANTIZE, and where it silently does nothing</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> QUANTIZE arrives over PRO DJ LINK and
        locks Beat FX to the grid, and it does not work on
        {NO_QUANTIZE_N} of them. AlphaTheta: "The QUANTIZE function cannot be used
        when [REVERB], [ROBOT], [MELODIC] or [SND/RTN(MIDI LFO)] is selected."
        There is no error and no indicator, you simply get un-quantised
        effects.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> A silent exception is worse than a
        refusal. You reach for a quantised REVERB, it lands where your finger
        landed rather than on the beat, and the obvious conclusion is that
        QUANTIZE is broken or that the Link is down. Neither is true. The
        {NO_QUANTIZE_N} exceptions are {NO_QUANTIZE}.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> There is a second condition that catches
        people out: "Music files must be analyzed with rekordbox in advance in
        order to use the QUANTIZE function." An unanalysed track has no grid to
        quantise to, so QUANTIZE does nothing for reasons that have nothing to do
        with the mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Building a transition around a
        quantised effect that is on the exception list. Assuming a drive exported
        without analysis will behave the same as your own prepared library.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx">Beat FX</a> <a href="#link-not-working">PRO DJ LINK is not coming up</a> <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox">rekordbox reference</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="peak-limiter">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Peak Limiter, and why it is already on</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> A master-output limiter added in firmware
        {DJM_900NXS.peakLimiterIntro} to alleviate "sudden, unpleasant digital
        clipping of the master output". It lives in CLUB SETUP as PKLIMIT, and its
        factory default is on.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> AlphaTheta states plainly that
        "by updating the firmware, Peak Limiter is enabled as default", so
        installing a firmware update changes how the desk sounds at the top of its
        range. That is unusual, and it is the reason an updated DJM-900NXS can
        sound different from the one you remember in the same booth.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> As a safety net in a room
        where you do not control every source, which is most rooms. It protects the
        PA and the audience from a sudden digital peak, and that is worth some
        headroom.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Treating audible limiting as a fault
        and hunting through the effects section for it. Turning it off to get
        loudness back instead of fixing the gain that is driving it.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#master-squashed">The master sounds squashed</a> <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="no-usb-storage">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">No drive goes into this mixer, and what that means</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> There is no USB storage role on this
        mixer at all. The one USB terminal is for a computer, and firmware goes in
        from a computer over a LAN cable into the LINK port. No stick, no card, no
        music playback from media and no recording to media.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters, and this is the interesting part.</strong> Every
        other Pioneer unit in this reference makes you think about drive format,
        and several of them punish the wrong answer. Here the question does not
        exist. {NOT_APPLICABLE_FS} are not unsupported on a DJM-900NXS, they are
        <strong>not applicable</strong>, because nothing is ever formatted for it.
        It is the one unit on this site where a DJ has no drive-format decision to
        make.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Recording is the analogue REC OUT, or over USB
        into a computer. If the plan for the night involves capturing the set,
        that decision is made at the laptop or the recorder, not at the mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Arriving with a firmware update on a
        USB stick, which is how every other Pioneer unit is updated and is not how
        this one is. Expecting a recording to appear on media afterwards.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#firmware-update">How the update goes in</a> <a href="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj">exFAT or FAT32, on the players</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="sound-card">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The built-in sound card, and a bit depth AlphaTheta states twice</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> One USB terminal for a computer, and a
        built-in 4-in / 4-out stereo sound card at {DJM_900NXS.samplingRate}. The
        asymmetry is documented and worth knowing: the four sets of input come from
        a single computer, while the four sets of output can be
        chosen from channels 1 to {DJM_900NXS.channels}, REC OUT, crossfader sides
        A and B, and the microphone.</p>
        <p><strong>Where the official record disagrees with itself.</strong>
        AlphaTheta publishes two different A/D figures for this mixer. The
        Operating Instructions describe a "{AD_MANUAL} high sound quality A/D
        converter and a {DA_MANUAL} high sound quality D/A converter", and the
        product page says "a {AD_PRODUCT} A/D converter". We are not picking one.
        Both are official, they contradict each other, and the honest position is
        that AlphaTheta's own documents disagree about the A/D stage.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> The separately routable
        crossfader sides are the useful detail here, because they let a DVS or
        recording setup capture the two sides of the crossfader independently
        rather than only the summed master.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Assuming a second computer can share
        the sound card. There is one USB terminal, and the input side is documented
        as coming from a single computer.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#dvs-and-software">DVS and software</a> <a href="#drivers">Drivers</a> <a href="#fx-noise-and-silence">USB audio noise</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="ins-and-outs">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Inputs, outputs and the specifications nobody publishes</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {DJM_900NXS.channels} channels, a
        {DJM_900NXS.eqBands}-band EQ isolator per channel, a crossfader with
        crossfader assign, {DJM_900NXS.micInputs} microphone inputs, MASTER1,
        MASTER2, a BOOTH output on TRS that takes balanced or unbalanced, REC OUT,
        and a coaxial DIGITAL IN. Discrete SEND and RETURN terminals for an
        outboard effector, and full MIDI controller capability.</p>
        <p><strong>One headphone output.</strong> AlphaTheta documents a single
        PHONES terminal, and this page is not going to imply a second one. The
        connector size, and whether a mini jack exists alongside it, are not
        recoverable from the documents we could read, so if you are packing an
        adaptor, pack both.</p>
        <p><strong>What is not published.</strong> AlphaTheta puts no specification
        table on the web for this model. Dimensions, weight, power consumption,
        frequency response, signal to noise ratio and distortion figures are not
        published in any page we could read, and the archive specification pages
        only point back into the Operating Instructions. So this page states none
        of them. If you need the dimensions for a flight case or a booth cutout,
        take them from the
        <a href={DJM_900NXS.sourceManual} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operating Instructions</a>
        rather than from any number on the internet, including ours.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Sizing a booth from a spec sheet found
        on a retailer's listing. Assuming the DJM-900NXS2's measurements carry
        over, which is a different product.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#send-return">The send and return loop</a> <a href="#sound-card">The built-in sound card</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="auto-standby">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Auto standby, and what is not documented about it</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The function exists, and there is a
        dedicated control to get out of it: the LFO FORM (WAKE UP) button, which
        AlphaTheta describes as "WAKE UP: Cancels the auto standby mode."</p>
        <p><strong>What is not documented, and we will not invent it.</strong> How
        long the desk waits before going into standby, whether it ships with the
        function on or off, and how to switch it off are not published in anything
        we could read. That information sits on a page of the Operating
        Instructions our tooling could not extract. We looked for it in support
        articles carrying a DJM-900NXS breadcrumb and found articles whose bodies
        were about entirely different products, so those were discarded rather than
        quoted. No timeout figure appears on this page, and none has been borrowed
        from another mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Treat the timing as unknown rather than
        generous. Touch a control during long gaps, and know where the LFO FORM
        (WAKE UP) button is before you need it in a dark booth.</p>
        <p><strong>One firmware note.</strong> No version introduces or changes
        auto standby. {DJM_900NXS.wakeUpFix} only fixes the case where cancelling
        it did not work.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#will-not-wake">It looks dead and will not wake up</a> <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="finished-firmware">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">A desk with no update waiting for it</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> {FLOOR}, from November 2016, is the
        version AlphaTheta offers, and nothing has followed it. That is not a
        criticism, it is a planning fact, and it changes the advice.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> On a maintained product, an odd
        behaviour is worth reporting and waiting on. Here, either the fault is in
        the list on this page and an update cures it, or it is behaviour you plan
        around. Read it as an observation rather than a verdict: no end-of-support
        statement exists for this mixer, and AlphaTheta has broken long silences
        before, shipping a CDJ-2000NXS version in 2024 after more than six years.
        Plan on nothing arriving, while knowing it is not impossible.</p>
        <p><strong>When professionals use it.</strong> Plenty of working rooms still
        run these, and a DJM-900NXS at {FLOOR} is a dependable desk with a known
        set of edges. The risk is not the mixer, it is assuming a fix exists for
        something.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Reading the {FLOOR} release date as
        the end of the product's life. It shipped roughly ten months after the
        DJM-900NXS2 was announced, so the two overlapped as supported products, and
        AlphaTheta names neither a predecessor nor a successor for this mixer.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#known-issues">Documented fixes, by firmware version</a> <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/djm-900nxs2">DJM-900NXS2</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="bulb" title="Professional tips" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="read-the-version">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Read the version, then decide what you will not use</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> Hold CUE in the BEAT EFFECT section and
        ON/OFF in the BEAT EFFECT section while you switch the power on, and the
        version appears on the display.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> On this mixer the version is not
        trivia, it is a list of what can happen to you tonight. Below
        {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix} the desk can switch itself off. Below
        {DJM_900NXS.wakeUpFix} it can refuse to come out of standby. Below
        {DJM_900NXS.effectsNotEngagingFix} there is no Peak Limiter and there are
        effects that misbehave when switched on. Below
        {DJM_900NXS.slipBpmDisplayFix} a linked player using SLIP can scramble the
        BPM readout.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Do it on arrival, in the same walk as checking
        routing and gain, and be specific with yourself about what you are giving
        up rather than hoping. If the desk is below {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix},
        raise it with the venue: that one is worth a conversation.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Reading the version and not writing it
        down. In a venue you play regularly, a note per booth is worth more than a
        note per gig.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#unexpected-shutdown">It switched itself off</a> <a href="/checklist">The pre-gig checklist</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="send-return">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">The send and return loop, and the 14th selector position</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> There are discrete SEND and RETURN
        terminals for an outboard effector, and the outboard loop is reachable from
        the Beat FX selector itself, as SND/RTN (MIDI LFO) in position
        {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions}.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> It means an outboard box behaves like
        one of the mixer's own effects rather than like a patch you have to think
        about, which is a good reason to bring a favourite reverb or delay to a
        residency. It is also why the effect count reads as
        {DJM_900NXS.beatFxCount} in one place and
        {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions} in another.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Test the loop before doors, with the outboard
        box set the way you will actually use it. And remember QUANTIZE does not
        apply to SND/RTN, so an outboard effect will not be beat-locked by the
        mixer.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Building a set around a quantised
        outboard effect. Leaving the selector on SND/RTN with nothing patched in
        and then wondering why the Beat FX section does nothing.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#beat-fx">Beat FX</a> <a href="#quantize">QUANTIZE</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="shield" title="Preventive maintenance" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="maintenance">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Keeping a desk this age reliable</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> One firmware pass to {FLOOR}, then it is
        connectors, cables and covers. There is no drive slot to keep clean and no
        media to fail.</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> A mixer that has been in a booth since
        the early 2010s has had a decade of cables levered in and out of it. Phono
        and line pairs, two microphone inputs, two masters, a booth output, a send
        and return loop, a coaxial digital input and a LAN port are all future
        intermittent faults if they get strained, and an intermittent input is the
        hardest thing on this page to diagnose during a set.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Unplug by the connector rather than the cable,
        make and break connections with the desk switched off, keep it covered
        between gigs, and write down the CLUB SETUP settings you rely on, PKLIMIT
        included, so a reset or a borrowed desk does not surprise you.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Do not coil cables tightly around
        their connectors, which is where strain damage starts, and do not leave the
        firmware update for gig day.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#firmware-update">How the update goes in</a> <a href="/prepare">Why gig day is a freeze</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="firmware-update">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">How the update goes in, over a LAN cable</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> From a computer, over the network, not
        from a USB stick. AlphaTheta's instruction is to "connect the LAN cable to
        the LINK port on the rear panel", run the updater on the computer, and
        "do not remove power cable and/or LAN cable during the update".</p>
        <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> This is the odd one out in the whole
        reference. Every other Pioneer unit here takes its firmware from a
        formatted stick, so a DJ or a technician who has done a dozen CDJ updates
        will arrive with the wrong thing and no LAN cable. There is no drive slot
        to put it in.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Allow time. Establishing the network can take
        about a minute, and AlphaTheta notes that if the Connect dialog keeps
        appearing for 30 seconds or longer, the network connection might not be
        established, which is a hub or a cable problem rather than a mixer problem.
        Do it between bookings, never on gig day, and leave the desk powered and
        cabled until the updater says it is finished.</p>
        <p><strong>Avoid it next time.</strong> Keep a known-good LAN cable and a
        hub you trust with the desk, because the update path and PRO DJ LINK depend
        on the same port and the same kind of hardware.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#no-usb-storage">No drive goes into this mixer</a> <a href="#link-not-working">PRO DJ LINK is not coming up</a> <a href="#read-the-version">Read the version</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="sync" title="Compatibility" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="compatibility">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Players and PRO DJ LINK</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> PRO DJ LINK over LAN into the LINK
        terminal, with up to {DJM_900NXS.maxPlayers} DJ players and
        {DJM_900NXS.maxComputers} computers when a switching hub is used. The three
        named Link functions on this mixer are {LINK_FUNCTIONS_LIST}.</p>
        <p><strong>The mixed-generation deduction, and it is ours not
        AlphaTheta's.</strong> In the QUANTIZE section the manual says "to use in
        combination with the CDJ-2000 or CDJ-900, first update the firmware to
        version 4.0 or later". That sentence does not say whose firmware. It cannot
        be the mixer's, which never went beyond {FLOOR}, so our reading is that it
        means the <strong>player's</strong> firmware. We are labelling that as a
        deduction rather than dressing it up as a quotation, because AlphaTheta
        never says it.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> The only other documented mixed-generation
        item is the BPM display fault fixed in
        {DJM_900NXS.slipBpmDisplayFix}, where SLIP on a linked CDJ-2000NXS or
        CDJ-900 scrambled the readout on this mixer. Beyond those two, nothing is
        documented about how this desk behaves with a CDJ-3000, a CDJ-2000NXS2 or
        any later player, so this page does not guess. Link is a LAN protocol and
        the audio path is analogue or digital audio, so plenty of these booths work
        fine, but "works fine in practice" is not the same claim as "documented"
        and we are not going to blur them.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#link-not-working">PRO DJ LINK is not coming up</a> <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/cdj-2000nxs">CDJ-2000NXS</a> <a href="/knowledge/pioneer-dj/rekordbox#which-player">Player compatibility table</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="dvs-and-software">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">DVS and software, with the conditions attached</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> All three DVS platforms, and every one of
        them carries a condition. rekordbox dvs needs the DVS Plus Pack. Serato DJ
        DVS is supported only with the Serato DJ Club Kit. TRAKTOR SCRATCH PRO 2
        and SCRATCH DUO 2 are <strong>certified</strong>, and that certified
        operation requires mixer firmware {DJM_900NXS.traktorFirmwareFloor} or
        later, which AlphaTheta writes as "1.280".</p>
        <p><strong>The word certified is doing real work there.</strong>
        AlphaTheta uses "certified" for TRAKTOR and "supported" for Serato DJ, and
        this page keeps that distinction rather than flattening both into one
        word.</p>
        <p><strong>One flat no.</strong> Serato SCRATCH LIVE is explicitly not
        supported. The product page's own words are "Doesn't support Serato SCRATCH
        LIVE". If that is your setup, this is a booking-stage problem, not a
        soundcheck one.</p>
        <p><strong>Common mistakes.</strong> Turning up with Serato DJ and no Club
        Kit. Turning up with TRAKTOR certified operation in the plan and a mixer
        below {DJM_900NXS.traktorFirmwareFloor}, which is the same version that
        fixed the unexpected shutdown, so it is a version worth checking for two
        reasons.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#read-the-version">Read the version</a> <a href="#sound-card">The built-in sound card</a> <a href="/knowledge/dictionary">DJ dictionary</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="drivers">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Drivers, and an operating system list that argues with itself</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <p><strong>Short answer.</strong> The macOS driver was last updated on
        {MAC_DRIVER.date}, at version {MAC_DRIVER.version}. The Windows driver was
        last updated on {WIN_DRIVER.date}, at version {WIN_DRIVER.version}. Both
        are still published for a mixer whose own firmware stopped in 2016, which
        is worth noticing.</p>
        <p><strong>Two documented failures worth carrying.</strong> Below Windows
        driver {WIN_DRIVER.floor}, "DJ applications running on certain versions of
        Windows 10 did not detect Pioneer DJ equipment". Below macOS driver
        {MAC_DRIVER.floor}, the "audio driver software wasn't recognized properly
        after restarting a Mac running macOS Big Sur". A laptop that boots and then
        cannot see the mixer is not a mixer fault.</p>
        <p><strong>Where the record disagrees with itself.</strong> AlphaTheta's
        operating system support list claims support through macOS Tahoe 26, while
        the Mac driver article for this mixer is titled "for macOS Ventura". That is
        AlphaTheta's inconsistency and we are reporting it rather than resolving
        it. If your machine is newer than Ventura, test the driver at home before
        you rely on it in a booth.</p>
        <p><strong>Do this.</strong> Boot the laptop, connect the mixer and open
        your software at home, in the state you will arrive in, and reboot once with
        the mixer connected. Then pack.</p>
        <p class="acc-related">Related: <a href="#sound-card">The built-in sound card</a> <a href="#dvs-and-software">DVS and software</a> <a href="/prepare">The full prevention system</a></p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <AccGroup icon="book" title="Frequently asked questions" />

    <details class="acc-sec" name="djm900nxs-acc" id="faq">
      <summary><h3 class="acc-h2">Quick answers</h3><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
        <dl class="rb-errors">
          <dt>What firmware should it be on?</dt><dd>{FLOOR}, published 15 November 2016. Nothing has followed it, so that is both the floor and the ceiling.</dd>
          <dt>Is it true that it can switch itself off?</dt><dd>Below {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix}, yes, and it is AlphaTheta's own documented fault: "an infrequently-encountered issue where DJM-900NXS unexpectedly shuts down". At {DJM_900NXS.shutdownFix} or above it is fixed.</dd>
          <dt>Why did nothing happen when I pressed the wake button?</dt><dd>Below {DJM_900NXS.wakeUpFix}, standby sometimes could not be cancelled with the LFO FORM (WAKE UP) button. That version fixed it.</dd>
          <dt>How long until it goes into standby, and can I turn that off?</dt><dd>Not documented. The function exists and the wake control is the LFO FORM (WAKE UP) button, but the timeout, the shipped default and the disable procedure are not published in anything we could read, and we are not borrowing a figure from another mixer.</dd>
          <dt>Why do I hear no effect when I cue Beat FX?</dt><dd>Because that is how it is meant to work with {CUE_DRY_LIST} selected. From {CUE_DRY_FROM} onward those {CUE_DRY_N} cue dry whether the effect is on or off.</dd>
          <dt>Why does the master sound compressed?</dt><dd>Peak Limiter, PKLIMIT in CLUB SETUP, added in {DJM_900NXS.peakLimiterIntro} and on by default once the firmware is updated.</dd>
          <dt>Does QUANTIZE work on every Beat FX?</dt><dd>No. It cannot be used with {NO_QUANTIZE}, with no error shown, and it needs tracks analysed in rekordbox first.</dd>
          <dt>Is it {DJM_900NXS.beatFxCount} Beat FX or {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions}?</dt><dd>Both. {DJM_900NXS.beatFxCount} effects inside the mixer, and a selector with {DJM_900NXS.beatFxSelectorPositions} positions because the extra one is SND/RTN (MIDI LFO) for an outboard effector.</dd>
          <dt>Does it read exFAT?</dt><dd>The question does not apply. No drive is ever inserted into this mixer, so {NOT_APPLICABLE_FS} are not applicable rather than unsupported. Firmware goes in from a computer over a LAN cable.</dd>
          <dt>Can I record my set onto a USB stick in the mixer?</dt><dd>No. Recording is the analogue REC OUT or over USB into a computer.</dd>
          <dt>Is the A/D converter 24-bit or 32-bit?</dt><dd>AlphaTheta's own documents disagree. The Operating Instructions say {AD_MANUAL} A/D with a {DA_MANUAL} D/A, and the product page says {AD_PRODUCT} A/D. We publish both rather than choosing.</dd>
          <dt>How many headphone outputs are there?</dt><dd>One documented PHONES terminal. The connector size is not recoverable from the documents we could read, so pack an adaptor.</dd>
          <dt>What are the dimensions and the weight?</dt><dd>AlphaTheta publishes no specification table on the web for this model, so we do not state them. Take them from the <a href={DJM_900NXS.sourceManual} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operating Instructions</a>.</dd>
          <dt>Does it work with Serato?</dt><dd>Serato DJ is supported for DVS with the Serato DJ Club Kit. Serato SCRATCH LIVE is explicitly not supported.</dd>
          <dt>Is TRAKTOR certified on it?</dt><dd>Yes, TRAKTOR SCRATCH PRO 2 and SCRATCH DUO 2, and certified operation needs mixer firmware {DJM_900NXS.traktorFirmwareFloor} or later.</dd>
          <dt>PRO DJ LINK will not come up. Is the mixer faulty?</dt><dd>Maybe not. AlphaTheta says some switching hubs may not operate properly and asks for a 100Base-TX-compatible hub, so the hub is a documented suspect.</dd>
          <dt>Did the DJM-900NXS2 replace it?</dt><dd>AlphaTheta names no successor and no predecessor for this mixer. Firmware {FLOOR} shipped roughly ten months after the NXS2 was announced, so the two were supported side by side.</dd>
          <dt>Will more firmware come?</dt><dd>Nothing has been published since November 2016, so plan on the basis that it will not. There is no end-of-support statement, and AlphaTheta did ship a CDJ-2000NXS version in 2024 after more than six years, so it is not impossible.</dd>
          <dt>Are documents titled DJM-900nexus the same mixer?</dt><dd>Yes. The Operating Instructions cover prints both names, "DJM-900NXS / DJM-900nexus", for one product.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </details>

